feat(personalization): give the frontend one governed personalization boundary

Add `src/types/personalization.ts` (split across a second file only because 300
lines is a hard rule), a `PersonalizationService` contract, and both runtime
implementations. React reaches personalization through `AgentService` and
nothing else; no component gains a Tauri import.

The types mirror the native DTOs as closed unions rather than bare strings, so
a screen gets exhaustiveness on statuses, scopes and warnings. Where two native
enums spell a multi-word value differently -- `rebuilding_derived` next to
`repair-required` -- the TypeScript reproduces the native rendering verbatim
and says why: a screen matching a string the other end never sends is silently
dead code.

The Web/mock adapter refuses what the desktop refuses. Revision conflicts on
policy and memory writes, reset tokens bound to the scope they were issued for,
a phrase that must match, cursors it did not issue, scopes missing the key they
are named after, and whitespace-only content all reject with the same message
strings the command layer sends -- which is the whole contract, since
`CommandError` serializes to its message and nothing else crosses the wire. A
permissive mock would leave a screen's conflict branch never once executed, and
that branch is the first one a real desktop hits.

The mock also honours session modes rather than reporting them: a temporary
session yields no eligible memory and no extraction, and a project-only session
drops global memories. A mock that returned the same counts for every mode
would let a mode selector ship having changed nothing.

The reset preview now returns its confirmation token as a named field instead
of a tuple. A tuple crosses the wire as a positional array, which is the one
shape a caller cannot read without knowing the order, and the token is useless
apart from the counts it authorises.

Two budgets move, both with the reason recorded beside them: `src/services`
19727 -> 20475 for 948 lines that are this capability's fixed cost at the
service boundary and duplicate nothing, and `agent-service.ts` 306 -> 308 for
the one import and one extends entry any new capability costs. Task 9.6 should
bring both back down.

Also add the `personalization` row to the three bounded-context maps, which
have been failing `docs:check` since the context was created.

Task 9.3, 9.4, 9.5.

